{"id":"ce6b2032-efce-47d6-89fe-cd8c40d8f7e3","object":"chat.completion","created":1785029957,"model":"deepseek-v4-flash","choices":[{"index":0,"message":{"role":"assistant","content":"{\n  \"implementation\": \"def solve(case):\\n    nodes = case['nodes']\\n    required_symbols = set(case['required_symbols'])\\n    # Build dictionaries\\n    node_by_id = {n['id']: n for n in nodes}\\n    children = {}\\n    for n in nodes:\\n        nid = n['id']\\n        pid = n['parent']\\n        if pid is not None:\\n            children.setdefault(pid, []).append(nid)\\n    # Initial retained set\\n    retained = set(node_by_id.keys())\\n    # Symbol counts\\n    from collections import Counter\\n    symbol_counts = Counter()\\n    for n in nodes:\\n        symbol_counts[n['symbol']] += 1\\n    # Sort nodes by depth descending, then id ascending\\n    sorted_nodes = sorted(nodes, key=lambda x: (-x['depth'], x['id']))\\n    removed_order = []\\n    for n in sorted_nodes:\\n        nid = n['id']\\n        if nid not in retained:\\n            continue\\n        if not n['removable']:\\n            continue\\n        if n['symbol'] in required_symbols:\\n            continue\\n        # Check for retained children\\n        has_retained_child = False\\n        for cid in children.get(nid, []):\\n            if cid in retained:\\n                has_retained_child = True\\n                break\\n        if has_retained_child:\\n            continue\\n        # Compute subtree (all retained descendants including node)\\n        subtree_nodes = []\\n        stack = [nid]\\n        while stack:\\n            cur = stack.pop()\\n            if cur in retained:\\n                subtree_nodes.append(cur)\\n                for child in children.get(cur, []):\\n                    if child in retained:\\n                        stack.append(child)\\n        # Compute symbol counts in subtree\\n        subtree_counts = Counter()\\n        for sid in subtree_nodes:\\n            subtree_counts[node_by_id[sid]['symbol']] += 1\\n        # Check if removing subtree would eliminate any required symbol\\n        can_remove = True\\n        for sym in required_symbols:\\n            if symbol_counts[sym] == subtree_counts[sym]:\\n                can_remove = False\\n                break\\n        if not can_remove:\\n            continue\\n        # Perform removal\\n        for sid in subtree_nodes:\\n            retained.discard(sid)\\n            symbol_counts[node_by_id[sid]['symbol']] -= 1\\n        removed_order.append(nid)\\n    retained_ids = sorted(retained)\\n    return {\\n        'removed_node_ids': removed_order,\\n        'retained_node_ids': retained_ids\\n    }\"\n}","reasoning_content":"We need to implement solve(case) for task SE-PE-01. The task: \"grammar_guided_node_reduction\" – reduce a parse tree by removing nodes while preserving required symbols and interestingness (utility? but not clearly defined). The problem description: Reduce a registered parse tree through grammar-valid deletion and replacement candidates while preserving an interestingness oracle. But we don't have explicit grammar or interestingness oracle. We have nodes with parent, removable, utility, depth, symbol. Required symbols list. The output should be removed_node_ids and retained_node_ids.\n\nThe algorithm likely: We have a tree. We want to remove some removable nodes, but we cannot remove nodes that are required symbols or the root. Also cannot remove a node that has retained children? Actually \"Never remove the root, a required symbol, or a node with retained children.\" So if a node's children are retained, we can't remove it. Also \"Tentatively remove a node only if all required symbols remain represented.\" So we need to ensure after removal, all required symbols are still present in the tree (somewhere). The algorithm: \"Consider deeper parse-tree nodes before shallower nodes.\" So process nodes in order of decreasing depth (deepest first). For each node that is removable, check if it is root? root is not removable anyway. Check if its symbol is in required_symbols? But required_symbols are symbols that must remain. So nodes with required symbol cannot be removed. Then check if node has any children that are retained? If we decide to remove a node, its children would be removed too? Actually \"Never remove ... a node with retained children.\" Means if we keep children, we can't remove the parent. So removal cascade: if we remove a node, its entire subtree is removed? Typically in parse tree reduction, removing a node means removing it and its subtree. So we need to ensure that after removal, all required symbols are still present somewhere in the tree (not necessarily under that node). So we can only remove a node if the required symbols are still present in other parts of the tree. Also we need to respect order: deeper nodes first. For nodes at same depth, use node ID as tie break.\n\nThe output: removed_node_ids (list of IDs in order of removal) and retained_node_ids (sorted retained IDs). We need to return sorted retained IDs (presumably alphabetically or based on ID string? The schema says array of strings, but not sorted explicitly. The description says \"Return sorted retained IDs\". So we should sort them.\n\nImplementation plan:\n\n- Build a dictionary mapping node id to node object.\n- Build parent-child relationships: for each node, if parent not null, add child to parent's children list.\n- Identify root: parent null.\n- Determine required symbols set.\n- Process nodes in order: sort by depth descending, then by node ID string for tie.\n- For each node in that order:\n  - If node is not removable, skip.\n  - If node id is root? root removable is false anyway.\n  - If node's symbol is in required_symbols, skip (cannot remove required symbol).\n  - Check if any child of node is still retained (i.e., not removed yet). But we are processing from deep to shallow, so children may already have been considered for removal. However, if a child is retained (not removed), then we cannot remove the parent. So we need to track which nodes are currently retained. Initially all nodes are retained. As we decide to remove a node, we mark it as removed and also consider its subtree removed. But we are processing from deep to shallow, so when we consider a parent, its children may or may not have been removed already. If a child is still retained, we cannot remove parent. If all children have been removed (or no children), we can consider removing parent.\n  - However, the rule \"Never remove ... a node with retained children\" implies we can remove a node only if all its children are also removed (or it has no children). So we need to check if any child is still present in the retained set. If yes, skip.\n  - Then tentatively remove: we need to ensure that after removing this node (and its subtree), all required symbols are still represented in the tree. That means for each required symbol, there exists at least one node (still retained) that has that symbol, and that node is not in the removed subtree. But since we are removing the whole subtree, we need to check that the required symbols are present elsewhere. So we can compute a map of symbol counts per symbol currently retained (excluding the subtree of this node). Or simpler: after removing the node, all required symbols must have at least one occurrence not in the removed subtree. We can have a global set of retained nodes. Start with all nodes. Then for each node considered, if removal conditions met, we would remove it and all its descendants. So we need to check if any required symbol would become absent. We can compute the set of symbols present in the subtree to be removed. Then for each required symbol, if it appears only in that subtree, removal would make it absent, so we skip. Otherwise, we can remove.\n  - If we decide to remove, we remove the node and all its descendants from retained set. Add node's id to removed_node_ids list (in order of removal). Then continue.\n- After processing all, the remaining retained node ids are the ones not removed. Sort them (string sort) and return.\n- Also need to output removed_node_ids in order of removal.\n\nEdge cases: What about nodes that are not removable? They are kept automatically. The root is not removable. Required symbol nodes are not removable. Non-removable nodes cannot be removed even if conditions satisfied? In the input, removable is boolean. Only nodes with removable true can be considered for removal.\n\nNow, the interestingness oracle: Not defined; presumably we don't have one. The task says \"preserving an interestingness oracle\". But the description also says \"validate every required field before computing a result\". The input has utility which might be related to interestingness? Not sure. The algorithm described is about grammar-valid deletion and replacement candidates. But the fixture shows only nodes with parent, depth, removable, utility, symbol. No grammar rules. Possibly the grammar is implicit: the tree structure and symbols define a grammar. But we aren't given any replacement candidates or grammar validity checks beyond the tree. The problem might be simplified; we just need to apply the given rules (required symbols, no removal of nodes with retained children, deeper first, etc.) and the utility might be irrelevant? Possibly utility is used as a heuristic for interestingness? But the problem statement says \"preserving an interestingness oracle\". However, the detailed instructions say \"Treat these registered properties as hard contracts rather than soft preferences: grammar_valid, interestingness_preserved, registered_candidate_order, stable_ties.\" That suggests we need to implement those as hard constraints. But we don't have oracle. I think the intended solution is to simulate the reduction algorithm with the given constraints. Utility might be used to decide which nodes to remove among removable nodes that have same depth? But the algorithm says \"Consider deeper nodes first\" and \"Use node ID as stable tie break\". That suggests depth and ID are the only ordering. So utility is likely not used in the decision to remove, but might be used to determine if interestingness is preserved? Not clear.\n\nGiven the input has utility, but the output schema only requires removed and retained IDs. The problem may be from a known task: \"grammar_guided_node_reduction\" where you need to remove nodes that are not required symbols and whose removal does not affect interestingness (defined as something like sum of utilities?). But the description says \"preserving an interestingness oracle\". Typically in Perses, interestingness is a binary property (the test case still fails). Here we have utility numbers. Could it be that we need to ensure that the sum of utilities of retained nodes is above some threshold? Not specified.\n\nGiven the limited info, I'll assume that the interestingness oracle is that the retained tree must still contain all required symbols (which we already enforce). That's a common reduction constraint: preserve certain symbols. So we ignore utility. The \"grammar_valid\" constraint is that the removal is valid only if the resulting tree is a valid parse tree under the grammar? But we don't have grammar rules. However, the tree structure itself with parent-child relationships is a parse tree. Removing a node also removes its subtree, which might break grammar if the parent expects children. But we are not replacing, just deleting entire subtrees. In a parse tree, deleting a node is like deleting that production. Usually in Perses, you can replace a subtree with a smaller one that still conforms to grammar. But here we only have delete. So perhaps it's simple: remove nodes that are removable, not required, and whose removal doesn't cause required symbols to vanish.\n\nGiven the test fixtures, I can try to infer. For case 0: nodes: root(symbol root), n1(parent root,s1), n2(parent root,s2,removable), n3(parent root,s0), n4(parent n2,s1,removable), n5(parent n3,s2), n6(parent n1,s0,removable). Required symbols: root, s1.\n\nLet's manually apply: Order by depth descending: depth 2: n4, n5, n6 (depth 2). n5 is not removable, skip. n4: removable true, symbol s1 (required) -> cannot remove because required symbol. n6: removable true, symbol s0 (not required), has no children? n6 has no children (depth 2 leaf). Check required symbols after removal: root still has root symbol, s1 still appears at n1 and n4 (but n4 is not removed yet). So s1 remains. So remove n6. Now removed: n6. Then depth 1: n1 (symbol s1, required, not removable anyway), n2 (removable, symbol s2 not required), n3 (not removable). n2: has child n4 (still retained). Since n4 is retained, cannot remove n2 because it has retained children. So n2 stays. n1 and n3 not removable. So final retained: root, n1, n2, n3, n4, n5. Sorted IDs: n1, n2, n3, n4, n5, root. Removed: n6.\n\nFor case 1: same structure. n6 removable, s0. Remove n6. n2 has child n4 (retained), can't remove. So same result.\n\nFor case 2: same structure. So expected output for all three: removed_node_ids=[\"n6\"], retained_node_ids = sorted list of all others. Sort: [\"n1\",\"n2\",\"n3\",\"n4\",\"n5\",\"root\"]? Actually root comes before n alphabetically? \"root\" vs \"n1\": 'r' > 'n'? In ASCII, 'r' (114) > 'n' (110), so root would come after n5? Actually \"n5\" vs \"root\": 'n' (110) vs 'r' (114), so 'n' < 'r', so n1-n5 come before root. So sorted ids: [\"n1\",\"n2\",\"n3\",\"n4\",\"n5\",\"root\"] for all three.\n\nBut we need to confirm the algorithm: \"Tentatively remove a node only if all required symbols remain represented.\" We did that. \"Never remove ... a node with retained children.\" We did that. So that seems to be the correct behavior.\n\nHowever, note the instruction \"Use node ID as the stable tie break at equal depth.\" That's for ordering among same depth. At depth 2 we had n4, n5, n6. n5 not removable, so order between n4 and n6: we need to process by node ID? Since depth same, we sort by node ID. That would be n4 < n6 (alphabetically: \"n4\" < \"n6\"). So n4 is considered first. n4 cannot be removed due to required symbol. Then n6 is considered and removed. That matches.\n\nSo implementation is straightforward.\n\nNow, I need to output a JSON with key \"implementation\" containing Python source as a string. The source must define solve(case). The function must take case (the payload object) and return a dict with removed_node_ids and retained_node_ids.\n\nImplementation steps in Python:\n\n- input case is a dict with \"nodes\" list and \"required_symbols\" list.\n- Build nodes_dict {node[\"id\"]: node}\n- Build children dict: for each node with parent, add node id to children[parent] list.\n- Identify root: node with parent None.\n- Build initial retained set = set of all node ids.\n- Build list of all nodes sorted by depth descending, then by id ascending (string sort).\n- removed_order = []\n- For each node in sorted list:\n  - if node[\"removable\"] is False: continue\n  - if node[\"id\"] in required_symbols? Actually required_symbols is a list of symbol strings, not ids. So check if node[\"symbol\"] in required_symbols set. If yes, continue.\n  - Check if any child of node exists in retained set: if any child_id in retained, then skip (cannot remove node with retained children).\n  - Now check if removing this node (and its descendants) would remove all required symbols. We need to check for each required symbol whether there is at least one node with that symbol outside the subtree. We can compute set of symbols present in subtree (all nodes in that subtree). Then for each required symbol, if it is only present in subtree and not elsewhere, then removal would lose it. So we need a global count of each symbol in retained set. But we are removing nodes one by one, and we only move forward. So we can compute symbol counts in current retained set. Then for the subtree, compute its symbol occurrence counts. If for any required symbol, its count in subtree equals its count in whole retained set, then removing that subtree would remove all occurrences, so skip. Else, we can remove.\n  - If removal is allowed:\n    - Gather all descendant ids of node (including node itself). We need subtree. Use DFS/BFS from node using children dict (only among retained? But children may have been removed? Actually if a child is already removed, it's not in retained. But since we process from deep to shallow, children deeper would have been processed before. If a child was removable and removed, it's not in retained. So the current node's children might be partially removed. But we only consider nodes that are still retained. So we should compute subtree of retained nodes? However, if a child was removed earlier, it's already gone. So the \"subtree\" of node to be removed includes only nodes that are still retained and are descendants of node. Since we process deep first, any descendant that could have been removed would have been removed already. So the set of descendants that are still retained is exactly the nodes that are in the retained set and are reachable from node via parent-child links. But we also have to consider that some descendants may have been removed earlier, but those are no longer in the tree. So removing node now would only affect currently retained descendants. So we need to collect all nodes in retained that are in the subtree of node. We can do a traversal starting from node, and include node id if it's in retained, then recursively include children that are in retained. But note: because we process deep first, all retained descendants will be deeper, so they haven't been removed yet. So it's fine.\n    - For each required symbol, compute counts in whole retained set (we can precompute a dict symbol_counts). Also compute counts in subtree. If for any required symbol, symbol_counts[symbol] == subtree_counts[symbol], then removal would eliminate that symbol, so skip.\n    - Else, remove: for each node_id in subtree (including node), mark as removed (remove from retained set), and add node_id to removed_order (but only the node itself? The output expects removed_node_ids list; it should list each removed node in order? Typically, when you remove a node, you remove its entire subtree. But should we list all removed nodes? The description: \"Return sorted retained IDs and the actual removal order.\" The removed_node_ids array should contain the IDs of removed nodes in the order they were removed. If we remove a subtree, we should list the parent node (the one we decided to remove) because that's the decision point. But also, its descendants that were still retained are removed as a consequence. Should we list those as separate entries? The description says \"Tentatively remove a node\" – it's about removing a node (the parent). The removal of descendants is implicit. But the returned list should include all removed nodes. However, if we list only the parent, we would not list the descendants that were removed as part of the subtree. But the output in the fixtures? We don't have expected outputs. We need to infer.\n\nLooking at the problem statement: \"Tentatively remove a node only if all required symbols remain represented.\" So we remove nodes one by one. When we remove a node, we remove its entire subtree. The children that were retained become removed. But in the processing order, children are deeper and will be processed before the parent. However, if a child is removable and we process it, we might decide to remove it individually. If we don't remove it, then when we get to parent, we might remove parent and that child along with it. So the final set of removed nodes includes both individual removals and collective removals. In terms of output, we need to list all node IDs that ended up removed. The order should be the actual removal order—the order in which we decided to remove nodes (the parent decisions). But if we remove a parent, its children are removed at that time, not earlier. So we should add only the parent to the removal order list, but the children were not individually removed; they are removed automatically. However, if we later check another node, it might be in the removed set and should be skipped. But the removal order list should reflect the chronological order of removal decisions. The problem says \"the actual removal order\". Given the algorithm, we only make removal decisions for nodes that we actively decide to remove. The subtree nodes are removed as side effect. So I think we should only add the node we decided to remove to removed_node_ids. And the descendants will be marked as removed but not listed in removed_node_ids (unless we also removed them individually earlier). But is that correct? Let's think: In typical Perses reduction, when you delete a node, you delete the entire subtree, and the node is considered \"deleted\". The children are not considered separately deleted; they are gone. The output would list the node that was deleted. But the problem says \"Return sorted retained IDs and the actual removal order.\" The removal order likely means the order in which we removed nodes (the decisions). So only the nodes we actively decided to remove should be in removed_node_ids.\n\nConsider the example: we have n6 at depth 2 removable. We decide to remove it. That's a removal. Then we later process n2 at depth 1. n2 has child n4 which is not removed. n2 has retained children (n4), so we cannot remove it. So only n6 is removed. So removed_node_ids = [\"n6\"]. That matches our earlier expectation.\n\nIf there were a case where a node's child was removable but not removed because of required symbol or other condition, then later the parent might be removed, removing that child as well. In that case, the child was never individually removed; it gets removed when parent is removed. The removal order should include only the parent's ID. So our algorithm should add the parent ID to removed_order only.\n\nBut what about nodes that are not removable? They cannot be removed. So they stay.\n\nThus, in our algorithm, when we remove a node, we add that node's id to removed_order list. We also mark all nodes in its subtree (including itself) as removed from retained set. Then later when we process other nodes, they will skip because they are either removed or have retained children.\n\nWe need to compute subtree. Since we are processing depth-first (deeper first), when we remove a parent, its children have already been processed (and possibly removed or kept). So the subtree we remove includes only nodes that are still retained at that moment. That's fine.\n\nImplementation details:\n\n- Children adjacency list: dict from parent_id to list of child ids.\n- Retained set: set of node ids initially all nodes.\n- Sorted nodes: use key = (-depth, node_id)\n- For each node, if node[\"id\"] not in retained: skip (already removed)\n- If not removable: skip (but we might still need to check if node is in retained? It is, so we just continue)\n- If symbol in required_symbols set: skip (even if removable, cannot remove)\n- Check if any child_id in retained: if yes, skip\n- Compute subtree nodes: use stack, starting from node, traverse children recursively that are in retained. Since retained set includes only nodes currently present. We need to collect all descendants that are in retained. But careful: if a child was already removed, it's not in retained, so it won't be included.\n- Compute symbol counts: we need a current symbol count for all retained nodes. We can maintain a Counter mapping symbol->count. Initially compute from all nodes. Then when we remove a subtree, we decrement counts for each node in the subtree.\n- Check: for each required_symbol, required_counts[symbol] in subtree_counts == current_counts[symbol]. If any equal, skip removal.\n- If allowed: remove all nodes in subtree from retained set, update symbol counts, add node[\"id\"] to removed_order list.\n- After loop, retained_node_ids = sorted(list(retained))\n- Return {\"removed_node_ids\": removed_order, \"retained_node_ids\": retained_node_ids}\n\nWe also need to handle nodes that are not removable but are required symbols: they stay. Fine.\n\nPotential issue: \"registered_candidate_order\" - The order of candidate nodes to consider is registered in input? Not given. We are using depth and ID as defined.\n\n\"stable_ties\" - Use node ID as tie break.\n\nNow, implement solve(case) accordingly.\n\nEdge Cases: If node has no parent (root), but root is not removable anyway.\n\nNow, write the Python source.\n\nWe need to return a JSON-serializable dict.\n\nMake sure to not mutate case.\n\nNow, output as JSON string in the \"implementation\" key.\n\nI'll write the code and then encode as JSON string.\n\nTesting with given fixtures: Should produce removed: [\"n6\"] and retained sorted.\n\nLet's write it."},"logprobs":null,"finish_reason":"stop"}],"usage":{"prompt_tokens":2057,"completion_tokens":5427,"total_tokens":7484,"prompt_tokens_details":{"cached_tokens":2048},"completion_tokens_details":{"reasoning_tokens":4828},"prompt_cache_hit_tokens":2048,"prompt_cache_miss_tokens":9},"system_fingerprint":"fp_8b330d02d0_prod0820_fp8_kvcache_20260402"}